<div class="faq-page">
  <h1>FAQ</h1>
  <p class="text-muted">Answers to the questions we hear most often. Something missing?
    <a class="text-accent" href="/contact/">Ask us</a>.</p>

  <div class="faq">

    <h2 id="general">General</h2>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="what-is-orcraft">What is Orcraft?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Orcraft is a platform for developing and orchestrating tests. It has two halves: a plain-Python
          <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/">test framework</a> that gives suites a defined structure, and
          a controller that runs those suites from a GUI, the command line or a CI pipeline, on the local machine
          or across remote endpoints, and collects the logs and results in one place.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="who-is-it-for">Who is Orcraft for?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Validation and engineering teams that test software, firmware or hardware on real machines: lab
          setups, devices under test, cross-platform automation and CI execution. If your tests need to run on
          more than one machine, be configured by people who did not write them, or produce results someone has
          to report on, Orcraft is built for you.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="code-first">What does "code-first" mean in practice?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>The code comes first and everything else follows from it. Suites, groups, tests, flows and steps are
          written directly in Python, and the controller reads that code to derive the configuration widgets in
          the GUI, the run plan, the CLI defaults and the CI snapshot. The suite is the single source of truth,
          so the GUI, the command line and CI can never drift apart.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="code-once">What does "code-once" mean in practice?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>You write a test suite once and re-run it under different settings without touching the code. A
          suite developed against localhost runs on remote endpoints by selecting them in the run panel. A
          parameter added to the configuration becomes a GUI field that anyone can change. Dimensions expand the
          same test into a different case matrix per run. The code stays the same; only the run configuration
          changes. An <a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/">AI agent</a> configures a run the same
          way without the panel: it redeclares the fields in a subclass, runs it and reads the log.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="code-only">What does "code-only" mean in practice?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>There is no second layer beside the code. No JSON, no YAML, no configuration files, no plugins and no
          scripts to keep in sync with the tests. Test configuration, including the widgets shown in the GUI, is
          declared in Python next to the test that uses it, and the controller generates the interface from that
          declaration at run time. If it is not in the Python, it does not exist.</p>
        <p>The controller does keep save files of its own, in the way an editor keeps a workspace file. A
          session is stored as a JSON file that you reopen, share or re-run, never write or edit by hand. A
          CI snapshot is exported as Python. Neither is something you maintain; both are produced from the code.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="new-language">Do I need to learn a new language or DSL?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>No. Orcraft has no DSL (domain-specific language) of its own. Tests are ordinary Python classes
          with a few base classes and field types imported from the <code>orcraft</code> package. Anything Python can do, a test step can do, including calling the
          libraries and tools your team already uses. Orcraft requires Python 3.14 or later.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="only-python">Does Orcraft only support Python?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Python is the native language of Orcraft: the framework is a Python library and tests are written as
          Python classes. That is where Python ends. A step is ordinary Python code, so it can launch an
          executable, drive a command-line tool, talk to firmware over a serial port, call a REST API, reach a
          device over the network or use a vendor SDK. The system under test can be written in any language, or
          not be software at all. Python is the wrapper that gives it structure, configuration and results.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="only-software">Does Orcraft only test software?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>No. Orcraft is built for validation labs, where the thing under test is as often a device, a board, an
          instrument or a complete system as it is a program. An endpoint is any machine that can reach the
          hardware: a lab PC wired to a device under test, a server on the same network as the instruments, a
          machine with the vendor's tools installed. Steps run there and talk to the hardware over whatever
          interface it offers. Software, firmware and hardware validation all use the same suites, the same GUI
          and the same results.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="is-ai-required">Is AI required to use Orcraft?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>No. AI-assisted generation and analysis are optional and stay dormant until you configure a provider.
          When enabled, the controller can generate a complete or partial suite from the documentation of the
          system under test, and analyze a run for failures, recurring patterns and recommended actions. Everything
          else works exactly the same without it.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="ai-providers">Which AI providers are supported?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, and any OpenAI-compatible gateway, including private ones inside your
          own network. Only the controller talks to the provider, and only when you ask it to. Endpoints never
          contact AI providers. See the
          <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/system-reference/#ai-providers">System reference</a> for what each
          provider needs.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="platforms">Which operating systems are supported?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Windows, Linux and macOS, for both the controller and the endpoints, and they can be mixed freely in
          one session. The controller GUI needs a desktop session. Endpoints have no GUI and run headless, on a
          server or over SSH. The validated versions are listed in the
          <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/system-reference/#operating-systems">System reference</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="need-endpoints">Do I need remote endpoints to get started?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>No. The controller runs suites on localhost out of the box, so you can develop and run everything on
          one machine. Endpoints are configuration, not code: a suite developed against localhost becomes a
          distributed suite by selecting endpoints in the run panel, with no change to the test body.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="data-leaves-network">Does any of my data leave my network?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Test traffic, logs and results stay between your controller and your endpoints. The controller makes
          two optional outbound calls, a version check against PyPI at startup and a status check for suites kept
          in Git, and both fail quietly when the network is unavailable. AI providers are contacted only if you
          configure one. Isolated machines can install from a copied wheel file.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="licensing">Is Orcraft open source? How is it licensed?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Orcraft™ is a commercial product of GM Core Ltd. and its source code is proprietary. It installs from
          PyPI with <code>pip install orcraft</code>. For licensing and pricing details,
          <a class="text-accent" href="/contact/">contact us</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="validated-with-orcraft">Orcraft is a test orchestration platform. Is Orcraft itself validated with Orcraft?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Hell yeah! Every component of Orcraft (the controller GUI, the framework, the endpoint
          application, the Maze app, the Tutorial suite and so on) is validated with Orcraft suites,
          written like any suite you would write. The tests are developed and enhanced continuously,
          and they run continuously.</p>
        <p>We see this as the strongest argument for the platform: if Orcraft can validate a system as
          complex as Orcraft, it can validate what you build.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <h2 id="why-orcraft">Why teams choose Orcraft</h2>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="ai-from-documentation">From documentation to a running suite, with AI</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Point the controller at the existing documentation of the system under test and it generates a
          complete or partial suite in the same plain-Python structure you would write by hand, ready to review
          and run. After the run, the same AI analyzes the results for failures, recurring patterns across
          endpoints and versions, and recommended actions. Both are optional, with the provider of your choice.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="single-source">One suite for GUI, CLI, CI and AI agents</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>The same Python suite runs from the controller GUI, from the command line, from a CI pipeline and
          from an <a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/">AI agent</a>. A run confirmed in the GUI is
          exported as a snapshot that reproduces it exactly elsewhere, so there is no "works in the GUI but
          not in CI". An AI agent runs the same suite by writing a file that sets the fields it needs, and
          reads the log afterwards.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="team-runs">The developer writes, the whole team runs</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Because the GUI is generated from the code, a lab technician or a tester who never opens the Python
          can select endpoints, set parameters, filter a matrix and run the suite. The developer decides what is
          configurable by declaring it; everyone else gets a form. Sessions and results are shared the same way.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="nothing-to-copy">Nothing to copy to lab machines</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>The controller uploads the test package to each endpoint at run time and updates the endpoint software
          whenever versions differ. Endpoints are registered once and can be shared between controllers and
          across the team.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="matrix">Case matrices instead of hand-written combinations</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p><a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/">Dimensions</a> expand one test into a
          planned matrix of cases, filtered from the GUI and executed in defined or randomized order over
          configurable cycles. The same test can also fan out to several endpoints, one independent run each,
          in parallel.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="results-in-one-place">Logs and results in one place</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Live logs from every endpoint, step-level statuses, statistics that roll up automatically from case
          to suite, spreadsheet export and portable session files. Sessions are saved, reopened later and
          re-executed with the same plan. With an AI provider configured, a run can be analyzed for failures,
          recurring patterns across endpoints and versions, and recommended actions.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="start-small">Start with one file, grow to a lab</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Every level of the hierarchy is optional. A single test file with a <code>main()</code> runs on its
          own, on localhost, with no group, suite or endpoint around it. The same file later joins a suite that
          runs across a lab full of endpoints, with nothing rewritten. There is no infrastructure to stand up
          before the first test runs.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="mixed-platforms">Mixed platforms in one session</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Windows, Linux and macOS, as controller or endpoint, in any combination within one session. Endpoints
          have no GUI and run headless on a server or over SSH. Everything installs with pip, so the toolchain is
          the Python, editor and Git your team already has.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="security">Secure by design</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Controller-to-endpoint communication can be secured with mutual TLS and token-based access control,
          so endpoints in remote labs or across an external network can join a session safely. The controller
          makes outbound connections only and needs no inbound firewall rule.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="suites-in-git">Suites live where your code lives</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>A suite can be kept in a Git repository or supplied as an archive. For Git-backed suites the
          controller checks the remote in the background and offers a fast-forward pull, without ever blocking
          a run.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <h2 id="comparison">How Orcraft compares</h2>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="vs-pytest">How is Orcraft different from pytest or unittest?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>pytest and unittest run test functions on one machine and report pass or fail. Orcraft covers what
          happens around that: a structured hierarchy of suites, groups, tests, cases, flows and steps, a GUI
          generated from the code so anyone can configure a run, dispatch of individual steps to remote
          endpoints, parametrized case matrices, and results that roll up from step to suite. Existing Python
          test logic can be called from an Orcraft step, so you do not have to throw anything away.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="vs-robot">How is Orcraft different from Robot Framework?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Robot Framework describes tests in a keyword language backed by Python libraries, so a team maintains
          both the keyword files and the code behind them. In Orcraft the test is the Python code, and the
          controller derives the configuration GUI, the run plan and the reports from it. There is no second
          layer to keep aligned, and no resource files to distribute to the machines that run the tests.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="vs-test-management">Does Orcraft replace a test management tool such as TestRail, Xray or Zephyr?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Not directly. Those tools track test cases, plans and results that are executed elsewhere. Orcraft is
          the execution side: it runs the tests, keeps every session with its live logs and step-level results,
          and exports structured spreadsheets and portable session files. Many teams find the saved sessions and
          exports cover their reporting needs; others keep their management tool and feed it from the exports.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="vs-ci">Does Orcraft replace Jenkins, GitHub Actions or GitLab CI?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>No, it runs inside them. A CI runner is a controller installation running in CLI mode, using a snapshot
          exported from the controller where the run was configured and confirmed in the GUI. The pipeline
          triggers the run; Orcraft handles distributing the package to endpoints, executing the plan and
          collecting results. You write no per-test pipeline glue.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="vs-sequencers">How does Orcraft compare to hardware test sequencers such as NI TestStand?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>These are the closest relatives: they also orchestrate sequences of steps against hardware and report
          on them. Orcraft differs in being Python-native and cross-platform, with the sequence living in code
          rather than in a dedicated editor and its own file format. It runs on Windows, Linux and macOS, dispatches
          steps to remote machines over the network, and installs with pip.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="vs-in-house">We already have in-house scripts. Why switch?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>In-house frameworks tend to grow a config format, a copy-to-lab-machine procedure, a results
          spreadsheet and a way to run in CI, each maintained by hand. Orcraft supplies all of that from the
          Python suite itself, so the team's effort goes into the tests. Because steps are plain Python, the
          scripts you have can usually be wrapped rather than rewritten.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <h2 id="getting-started">Getting started</h2>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="how-long">How long does it take to get a first test running?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Minutes. Install Python, run <code>pip install orcraft</code>, start the controller and run the
          Hello World suite from the <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/">Tutorial</a> on localhost. Remote
          endpoints, matrices and CI come later, one chapter at a time.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="endpoint-install-time">How long does it take to install an endpoint?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>It depends on whether you want a secure connection. An
          <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/endpoints/#insecure-endpoint-connection">insecure endpoint</a>
          takes a few minutes: install Python, run <code>pip install orcraft</code>, start the endpoint with
          security disabled and add it in the controller by name or IP address. It is the fastest way to try
          remote execution on a trusted network.</p>
        <p>A <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/endpoints/#secure-endpoint-connection">secure endpoint</a> adds
          the security setup procedure in the controller's Security tab, which generates the authorization token
          and the certificates for mutual TLS, and a firewall rule on the endpoint. Plan on about 15 minutes for
          the first one. An endpoint added without security can be switched to secure later by editing it in the
          controller, so you can start fast and harden afterwards.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq__item">
      <summary class="faq__question"><h3 class="faq__title" id="see-it">Can I see it before installing?</h3><span class="faq__marker">{% icon "chevron-right" %}</span></summary>
      <div class="faq__answer">
        <p>Yes. The <a class="text-accent" href="/demo/">Demo</a> page shows the controller in action, and the
          <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/">Tutorial</a> walks through developing, configuring, running and
          analyzing a suite with screenshots.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

